 #:seon.task{:name "reply-size-prints-sources-to-measure-them",
            :plan
            "`seon.cluster.agent/submit-source-in-projection` still computes `:seon.turn/reply-size` by printing sources and counting that text. Carry the size from the authored request text without printing its parsed forms. Keep reply-size's consumer and units explicit; a regression must show that measuring the reply never prints or realizes its values. Read `seon.cluster.agent/submit-source!` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-read-notes.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "finished-agents-and-their-branches-accumulate"
             :plan "Default holds 185 agents, each with a `:seon.agent/branch` (measured through JVM eval_clj on default: 185 `:seon.agent/id` entities, 185 branch values), and bin/seon start --head captured about 110 agent branch heads at a restart. Agents from finished lanes and probes are never archived and their branches never unlinked, so every restart captures and restores them. Related: my.agent/retire-branch-refuses-a-finished-agent-as-admission-closed (retire at run exit). Done when a finished agent's branch is unlinked and the agent archived at its run's exit, and the agent count on default after a probe batch returns to its prior value. Read `my.agent/retire-branch!` and `seon.cluster.registry/retire-branch!`."}
#:seon.task{:name "two-plan-passes-start-one-attempt"
             :plan "Card R1 (r1-db-flow-design.md, hybrid-start-a-flow.md H6/H7). `seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow` (the one Flow proc in the cluster graph linking Datahike and Flow) reads `seon.cluster.agent/launch-facts` on main (`seon.task/ready`, open attempts with their task's intent, ended counts) and the registry, asks the pure `seon.cluster.agent/launch-actions` for actions and applies each: `:attempt` writes the attempt and its agent in ONE main transaction (`seon.task/attempt-tx` + `seon.cluster.agent/creation-tx`) before anything runs, `:start-flow` calls `seon.cluster.agent/start-flow!` (fork from main, world, environment, graph paused, resumed only under :running). A second pass writes nothing and starts nothing; `:seon.config.agent/max-running` (default 4) counts open attempts. Nothing else starts a graph: `seon.cluster.host/host!` and the per-branch db<->flow graph are deleted. Proofs: seon.cluster.db-flow-test/plan-writes-the-attempt-first-and-makes-flow-follow (the table), the two-step chain test. Read `seon.cluster.registry/fork!`, `seon.context/current-state`, `seon.flow/start-graph!`, `seon.profile/measure`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/r1-db-flow-design.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "each-intent-names-one-roster-state"
             :plan "Card R2. Intents to Flow verbs: :paused launches without resume or pauses a running graph; :running resumes or re-hosts; :stopped pauses, joins, stops and releases the SCI context keeping agent, branch and attempt; :cancelled interrupts, ends :cancelled with a reason and deletes the branch; cluster :paused wins; `seon.flow/start-graph!` resumes only under the intent. A table over the five intents. (hybrid-done-and-merge M12, reused P8) Close derivations in `next-actions`: `:cancel` (intent `:cancelled`: interrupt, await actual exit, then `close-tx` writes outcome `:closed` with end `:cancelled` and `:delete-branch`; no task for root, no end written by `seon.cluster.agent/follow!`), send-back, cap, stall, given-back and error closes with the end the branch holds at C; stop keeps the branch and the open assignment; pause is acknowledged by `clojure.core.async.flow/ping-proc`. Red proofs: `seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-cancelled-assignment-closes-without-a-task-for-root` (`:closed`, end `:cancelled`, zero new root tasks) and the filed deftest tagged to this task (`each-intent-names-one-roster-state`). Cost: `close-tx` under 10 ms. After M5. Read `seon.cluster.agent/apply-actions!`, `seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow`, `seon.cluster.agent/launch-actions`, `seon.cluster.agent/start-flow!`, `seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow!`, `seon.task/close!`, `seon.turn/end-task-call` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-done-and-merge.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "disarm-splits-into-stop-and-cancel"
             :plan "Card D3 (with R2). design-probes P3: `clojure.core.async.flow/stop` then `seon.flow/join-graph!` without `interrupt-turn!` exits after the current transform (Flow reads control with priority between transforms; a transform is never preempted), and `flow/pause` then `flow/ping-proc` under the same bound acknowledges the pass's end. So `seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow!` splits: a :stopped intent is `stop-graph!` minus the interrupt-turn! line, a :cancelled intent keeps it; the bound `:seon.config.agent/turn-completion-backstop-ms` covers a whole pass (its dial already adds the provider or evaluation limit); a pass past the bound gets today's typed join timeout with the entry kept. Proof: R2's table row for :stopped (seon.cluster.agent-test/each-intent-names-one-roster-state). Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/design-probes.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-one-fork"
             :plan "Phase E3 of hybrid-immutable-env.md (H5 option B, decided). A fresh agent's environment is ONE `sci.core/fork` of the frozen program context (`seon.sci.eval/program-context`, phase E2) plus custody, print session, fresh kernel atoms and result objects: `seon.sci.eval/fork-for-agent` (value in, context out) replaces `seon.sci.eval/copy-base-ctx`, `seon.sci.eval/fork-cluster-ctx`, `seon.sci.eval/branch-ctx` and `seon.sci.eval/fork-for-turn`'s fresh path at `seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context!`, `seon.test`'s request world and `seon.cluster.boot`; the agent keeps the value it forked from; `:my.program/base-ctx` and `:seon.sci.eval/base-ctx` leave the handle. Lazy installs (`seon.sci.kernel/ensure-function!`) land in the agent's own fork; the proof records how many functions the loaded value leaves lazy, deciding whether the derivation installs them eagerly. Proof: seon.cluster.agent-test/a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-two-forks renamed a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-one-fork and tightened: `seon.sci.eval/inherit-var-bindings!` called once, `seon.sci.eval/acquire!` and `seon.sci.eval/fork-cluster-ctx` zero, no history reads for an agent that never ran, custody is the branch connection, environment leg under 1 ms and 1 MB beyond the lookup (14.5 ms and 9.1 MB with two forks, 25.6 ms and 16.6 MB with four). Files: src/seon/sci/eval.clj, src/seon/cluster/agent.clj (held by lane p4-branch-writes: a patch for root), src/seon/test.clj, src/seon/cluster/boot.clj, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj. Read `seon.profile/measure`, `seon.sci.eval/base-ctx`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-immutable-env.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-received-message-is-written-on-the-recipients-branch-and-rendered-in-its-context"
             :plan "Phase AW7 of hybrid-agent-world.md (Opus lane; beside AW4 and AW5). Messages are channels: the agent graph's mailbox proc (`seon.cluster.agent/mailbox-step`, today one payload-free wake in-port on a sliding-buffer 1) gains a second in-port :seon.agent/inbox, a bounded buffer carrying the message value (new dial :seon.config.agent/inbox-size, default 64, resources/seon/schemas/seon.config.agent.edn), registered in the registry beside the wake channel so `seon.cluster.agent/channels` answers it for `seon.cluster.message/send!`'s offer!. On an inbox message the :io transform writes the message on the agent's OWN connection with `seon.cluster.message/send-call` (identity :seon.message/id, so a repeat is a no-op) and then emits its :seon.agent/episode signal, so the next turn's `seon.context/current-state` renders the inbox from the agent's own branch as today. Nothing is written in transit, nothing relays through main, no other writer touches the branch; a crash between offer and write loses the message (channels are ephemeral), and the sender resends by the same id. Root's graph has the same mailbox, so messages to root are written on main by root's own proc. Proofs: this test (after one offer the recipient's branch holds the message by identity, main and the sender's branch do not, the episode fired once and current-state shows the message; offer -> write under 30 ms warm through seon.profile/measure); a-resent-message-with-the-same-id-writes-nothing (two offers, one datom set, two episodes); a-message-to-root-takes-the-same-path (root's mailbox writes it on main). Files: src/seon/cluster/agent.clj (mailbox-step, graph-definition, routing), resources/seon/schemas/seon.config.agent.edn, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj. Read `seon.cluster.agent/graph-definition`, `seon.profile/measure` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-agent-world.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-real-chain-runs-with-no-nudges"
             :plan "(hybrid-done-and-merge M-final) After M1-M18 are merged and default restarts on that HEAD, the chain test `seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-green-first-step-ends-itself-and-its-end-starts-the-next-from-its-commit` is extended to Design+Prove (an expected red product) then Implement then auto-merge, with no hand end edits, no copying of proofs to main and no successor launched by hand (injected provider replies); then chain experiment 1's subject (`seon.sci.forgive/segments`, trailing prose) reruns paid with the same instructions: about 5 + 4 calls, at most one send-back, merged into main with zero nudges. Files: `test/seon/cluster/db_flow_test.clj`; the run's note under `docs/research/agent-platform/`. After M18 (`seon.vocabulary/the-merge-path-names-no-retired-identifier`). Read `seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow`, `seon.cluster.agent/launch-actions`, `seon.context/current-state`, `seon.turn/task-end` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-done-and-merge.md`."
             :needs #{[:seon.task/sym seon.vocabulary/the-merge-path-names-no-retired-identifier]}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-requested-green-end-merges-and-publishes-with-no-hand-act"
             :plan "(plan.md §0 item 1, M8a, after M9 `seon.merge/an-accepted-merge-is-published-by-the-next-pass`) `seon.cluster.agent/next-actions` merges a branch holding the agent's merge request like any other done end: `:merge` (`seon.merge/merge!`, whose `seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge!` gate tests the request's proofs and the tests reaching the change, with the files proven against `seon.merge/checkout`), then `:publish`, then `:delete-branch`. Deleted: `:seon.flow.actions/requested?` (schema key, `seon.cluster.agent/branch-end`'s request read, the clauses in `seon.cluster.agent/lifecycle-facts` and `next-actions`) and the `:close` it forced, with root's notice task naming `my.root/merge!` for a requested end. A refused gate is the assignment's `:error` end with nothing merged. Red proof: `seon.cluster.merge-test/a-requested-green-end-merges-and-publishes-with-no-hand-act` (an own-branch agent's green end and merge request: one pass merges, the next publishes into the checkout as its new HEAD). The proof `seon.task/a-done-branch-merges-with-no-merge-request-written-by-the-agent` stays with M8. Read `seon.merge/publish!`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-audit-2.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/prebrief-m9-m8a.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-released-lane-retires-its-agent-branch"
             :plan "(owner, 2026-10-12, M13 default 2) `bin/lane release LANE` retires the lane's agent branch: it writes the lane agent's end, `:seon.agent/admission-closed-tx`, through `my.agent/retire-branch!` of the agent's own branch, so root's next `seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow` pass deletes the branch when nothing is unmerged (`seon.cluster.agent/next-actions`' `:delete-branch`) and lists it otherwise. Today a released lane's agent branch stays until a hand retirement. Read `seon.cluster.agent/lifecycle-facts`, `bin/lane` and `docs/seon/specs/agent-loop.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-end-kind-table-decides-which-ends-read-work-and-allowance"
  :plan "`seon.cluster.agent/lifecycle-facts` acquires `:seon.flow.actions/work-left?` and `:seon.flow.actions/allowance-left` only when its `judged?` holds, a hand set `#{:relaunch :close}` over `seon.task/end-kind`'s kinds (595f8f7de), while `seon.cluster.agent/assignment-actions` decides relaunch and close from the same kinds and `:seon.flow.actions/refused?`: one classification written twice, so a new kind or a changed rule drifts between them. Done when the kinds whose actions read work and allowance are declared once beside `seon.task/end-kind` (its table, or one function both read) and `lifecycle-facts` holds no literal kind set. Red proof to write before assignment: a row in `seon.cluster.db-flow-test/ends-share-one-action-owner` where a refused `:done` end (kind `:merge`) relaunches with work and allowance read, failing if the acquisition and the action rule disagree. Read `docs/seon/schedule/cards/TASK-CHAIN.md#interfaces`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-failed-actions-ownership-is-declared-with-the-action"
  :plan "`seon.cluster.agent/failed-pass!` decides which failed actions end their assignment `:error` with a hand set `#{:attempt :start-flow :merge-base :merge}` over `:seon.flow.actions/op` (held since before 595f8f7de), so an op added to `seon.cluster.agent/assignment-actions` is silently excluded. Done when whether an op's failure ends its assignment is declared once with the ops (the `:seon.flow.actions/op` enum's member properties or the action owner) and `failed-pass!` reads it; the set literal goes. Red proof to write before assignment: a `seon.cluster.db-flow-test` case where a `:relaunch` or `:publish` action throws and the assertion names the declared outcome. Read `seon.cluster.agent/next-actions`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "basis-valid-is-reused-across-unrelated-main-commits"
             :plan "`seon.cluster.agent/basis-valid?` caches under `[(db/commit-id database) sym]` via `seon.cache/lookup ::basis-valid`, so every main commit, related or not, makes every ready leaf's obligations cold. Measured on default with 834 ready leaves (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/reactive-scheduling.md#2-measurements-default-warm-read-only`): `seon.cluster.agent/launch-facts` 355-370 ms and 341 MB warm, 1.3-2.3 s and 1.4 GB cold; `basis-valid?` alone 1,292 ms and 1,432 MB cold against 3 ms warm, about 1.55 ms and 1.7 MB per ready leaf. What the work must be proportional to: the leaves whose task, ancestors, needs or proofs (what `seon.task/obligations` reads) changed since the last pass, not the leaves times the commits. Key the cache by the content `seon.task/obligations` read (its read evidence or the digests of those entities) so an unrelated commit reuses the value. Proof: to write before assignment (`seon.profile/measure` on `seon.cluster.agent/launch-facts` after one unrelated main commit, on the same 834-leaf probe, against the cold figure)."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-idle-lifecycle-pass-reads-taskless-agents-in-proportion-to-what-changed"
             :plan "`seon.cluster.agent/taskless-agents` is 94% of an idle `seon.cluster.agent/lifecycle-facts` (11.4 of 12.1 ms and 13.0 MB of 17.3 MB, with one assignment and 3 taskless agents, empty registry; `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/reactive-scheduling.md#2-measurements-default-warm-read-only`, finding 3 of `#5-findings-to-file-not-mine-to-fix`). It reads each taskless agent's end and branch head on every pass whether or not anything changed. What the work must be proportional to: the agents whose branch head moved since the last pass, with an unchanged agent answered from its record by head commit id. Change the owner so an idle pass reads no taskless agent's branch. Proof: to write before assignment (`seon.profile/measure` on `lifecycle-facts` with 3 unchanged taskless agents, under 2 ms)."}]
